// Lesson 10.2c — Decks: Shuffle, Draw & Traverse
// rpg jam: implement Shuffle() (the two empty overloads); Fisher-Yates + AP swap.
//
// WHICH PROJECT — this lesson does NOT run on AP_CS_P_Starter_Project (Units
// 1-9, Unity 6000.6.0f1). The Unit 10 card jam runs on
// Card-Game-Template-main (Unity 6000.4.0f1), and that project is the only one
// with a GameManager.cs, a Card_data.cs or a Shuffle() in it at all. Verified by
// reading Card-Game-Template-main/Assets/Scripts/, which holds exactly four real
// scripts: GameManager.cs (81 lines), Card_data.cs (16), Card.cs (54),
// DraggableUI.cs (31).
//
// GROUND TRUTH (re-read before every edit to this page):
//   - GameManager.cs: singleton `public static GameManager gm;` set in Awake()
//     with a duplicate guard + DontDestroyOnLoad. SIX List<> fields, and they
//     are TWO different element types: player_deck / ai_deck are
//     List<Card_data> (the ScriptableObject assets from 10.2b);
//     player_hand / ai_hand / player_discard_pile / ai_discard_pile are
//     List<Card> (the components on screen). That split is the reason Shuffle
//     needs two overloads, and it is taught, not hidden.
//   - `Shuffle` has TWO overloads — void Shuffle(List<Card_data> data_hand) and
//     void Shuffle(List<Card> card_hand) — and BOTH BODIES ARE EMPTY in the
//     starter. `AI_Turn()` is empty too (10.2d's business).
//   - `Deal()` is real working code: it calls Shuffle(player_deck);
//     Shuffle(ai_deck);, then Instantiates blank_card into the canvas at
//     player_hand_location / ai_hand_location, assigns
//     GetComponent<Card>().data = player_deck[0] / ai_deck[0], and disables
//     DraggableUI on the player's card. It deals exactly ONE card to each side,
//     from index [0].
//   - `Deal()` as shipped NEVER puts anything into player_hand or ai_hand — it
//     instantiates a visual and drops the reference. So the hands are empty
//     lists and any loop over one renders nothing. This page says so out loud
//     (step 6) rather than telling a student to traverse a list that is always
//     empty. Draw() is what makes the hand a real list for the first time.
//   - `Card_data.cs`: the ScriptableObject — [CreateAssetMenu(...)], fields
//     card_name, description, health, cost, damage, sprite, int fontSize = 36.
//   - `Card.cs`: holds `public Card_data data;`, copies data.* into TMP text
//     fields in Start(), and ends with a hardcoded
//     `if (card_name == "Orc") background.color = Color.chocolate;`. Because
//     Start() dereferences `data`, a card visual instantiated without an
//     assigned Card_data throws a NullReferenceException in Card.Start() — a
//     real pitfall, and one the walkthrough's `Draw` step warns about.
//   - `DraggableUI.cs`: the drag behaviour; disabled on the player's dealt card.
//
// THE COMPILE DEFECT THIS LESSON HAS TO FIX (and turns into the teaching moment)
//   GameManager.cs line 3 is `using Unity.Mathematics;`, and `com.unity.mathematics`
//   1.3.3 IS installed in this template — not in Packages/manifest.json directly,
//   but pinned in Packages/packages-lock.json at depth 2 (verified), and it
//   defines Unity.Mathematics.Random. UnityEngine also defines Random. So inside
//   GameManager.cs a bare `Random.Range(...)` is CS0104: "'Random' is an ambiguous
//   reference" and the file does not compile.
//   The AP/C# side-by-side in the Concept Lab therefore prints the QUALIFIED call,
//   `UnityEngine.Random.Range(0, i + 1)`, and the one blank line in the walkthrough
//   is that call — so the ambiguity is the lesson, not an unexplained error a
//   student hits after pasting. It is the Concept Lab's callout trap, and the
//   `hint` and `why` on the walkthrough's blank line.
//
// THE ALGORITHM, VERIFIED BEFORE TEACHING IT
//   AP:  FOR i FROM LENGTH(list) TO 2 / r <- RANDOM(1, i) / SWAP(list[i], list[r])
//   C#:  for (int i = list.Count - 1; i > 0; i--) / int r = Random.Range(0, i + 1);
//   - AP lists are 1-indexed, C# lists are 0-indexed, so AP's i runs LENGTH..2 and
//     C#'s runs (Count-1)..1. The two ranges are the same slots.
//   - Random.Range(int, int) is MAX-EXCLUSIVE, so Range(0, i + 1) yields 0..i
//     inclusive — which is what Fisher-Yates needs. Range(0, i) yields 0..i-1 and
//     the slot at i can never be picked, so the last card never moves: a shuffle
//     that is wrong in a way that still LOOKS shuffled. The reference sheet's
//     RANDOM(a, b) row already prints this AP/Unity difference; this page says it
//     in a `why` as well.
//   - Deal() reads deck[0] after calling Shuffle, so a broken shuffle shows up as
//     "the same card is always dealt first" — named in the walkthrough's last
//     step as the observable symptom.
//
// Scaffolding: GUIDED (level 2), and asks.ts agrees ("how do I shuffle a deck and
// draw from it?", level 2). The file's shape is fully given; exactly TWO lines are
// gaps, and both carry this lesson's genuinely new idea: the random-slot call (which
// is also where the namespace ambiguity lives) and the empty-deck guard. Everything
// around them — the loop, the swap, the Add/RemoveAt, the index traverse — is
// 10.2a's list vocabulary and 10.2b's assets, used for real.
//
// PROTECTED BLOCKS — answer ids are POSITIONAL (lessonId:shortCode:N:qN, see
// src/lib/formify.ts), so nothing here is reordered, renumbered, added or removed.
// The one block this page renders, in document order and with its counts
